who does it themselves.

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:09.635
And it's very big of you
to admit it.

00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:11.503
-Listen, I think it's a story

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.473
that needed to be told
for a long time.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.740
[ Laughter ]

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:16.409
It's going to be
a big expose on Netflix.

00:01:16.475 --> 00:01:18.044
It's about the pant people.

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:20.080
It's a whole part of the
subculture of the industry.

00:01:20.146 --> 00:01:21.314
Yeah, I'm going deep here
with this.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:22.549
-You're going deep.
Yeah.

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.417
I'm going deep
with this riff, um...

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.053
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.021
The pant people need to be
recognized.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:30.256
-They need to be recognized.

00:01:30.323 --> 00:01:31.691
-You know who's hard
to find?

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:33.225
Uh, corduroy guys.

00:01:33.292 --> 00:01:34.994
Because it's a whole --
You really need three guys

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:36.496
for a pair of corduroy.

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:38.831
-Back in the '70s when,
you know, we were 30.

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:40.767
-Yeah.
-Um.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.236
Those guys --
Those guys got recognized.

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:45.705
But these days,
they're just disposed.

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:46.973
-Yeah, it's true.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.274
Thank you for a shout
out to the pant people.

00:01:48.341 --> 00:01:49.575
-All right.
Thank you.

00:01:49.642 --> 00:01:51.677
-Um, hey, also, Happy New Year.
-Hey.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.946
-I mean, first guest of 2024.
It means a lot.

00:01:54.013 --> 00:01:55.347
-Hey, I first footed you.

00:01:55.414 --> 00:01:57.416
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:57.483 --> 00:01:58.651
It means a lot to me,
actually.

00:01:58.717 --> 00:01:59.952
Actually,
they told me that and I was --

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:02.889
I was, uh, I was I
was impressed with myself.

00:02:02.955 --> 00:02:06.659
Um. Uh, yeah.
I've literally first footed you.

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:07.960
-Oh, first footed. Okay.

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:09.895
Now, I don't know
what that means.

00:02:09.962 --> 00:02:11.430
-Er.
[ Chuckles ]

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:13.265
So in Scotland, where --

00:02:13.332 --> 00:02:14.667
[ Heavy accent ] where
I come from, in my country.

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:17.069
[ Normal voice ] It is, um,
which is not the accent

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:20.640
of my country, um,
or possibly any country.

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:21.875
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:25.578
Uh, the first footing tradition
is the, um,

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:27.479
the first person
that comes into your house

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.749
needs to carry something.
-On the new year?

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.150
-Yeah.
-So after midnight.

00:02:31.216 --> 00:02:32.618
-After midnight,
after the bells.

00:02:32.684 --> 00:02:36.322
We call it the bells.
When -- When New year strikes,

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:37.857
the first person
that comes into your house

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:39.225
has to be bringing
something good.

00:02:39.292 --> 00:02:42.362
Like some food, some coal,
traditionally coal

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:43.997
to keep your house warm
and to keep you fed.

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:45.598
And if the first person
that comes into your house

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:48.835
doesn't have these things,
bad luck ensues.

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:52.772
So what ends up happening is
that whoever is in your house,

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:55.174
like your child,
or your best friend,

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.909
or your, I don't know,

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.678
the wife of your child
or something like that,

00:02:59.745 --> 00:03:01.013
someone you don't care
about very much

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.016
has to go outside in the cold.

00:03:04.083 --> 00:03:05.285
And when everybody else

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.086
is cheering
and singing "Auld Lang Syne"

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.654
and kissing and things
like that,

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.456
they're standing outside
in the snow,

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:15.928
freezing, holding a lump of coal
and a glass of drink.

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:18.798
And -- And as long
as in the new year,

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:21.000
they're the first person
to cross your threshold,

00:03:21.066 --> 00:03:22.668
they bring good luck.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:25.071
-Gotcha. So you're basically
like you're fixing the game.

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.440
You're like, we're going
to put some good luck outside.

00:03:27.506 --> 00:03:29.809
-You're you're playing the man
at his own game.

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:32.178
-And it does seem like a lousy
job because it's cold.

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:33.346
-Yeah. It sucks.

00:03:33.413 --> 00:03:35.581
-And how early do
they send you out?

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:37.716
-Uh, it's only like five,
ten minutes,

00:03:37.783 --> 00:03:38.918
but you miss the good bit.

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:40.520
I remember the first time
my wife came to

00:03:40.586 --> 00:03:42.321
Glasgow for New Year.

00:03:42.387 --> 00:03:45.224
Um, all the men
had to go outside

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.892
so that the men
were first footing

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:48.460
the women,
which is great.

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:49.962
You know, all the women
got first footed.

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.397
Sounds terrible doesn't it?

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:52.999
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:54.533
It sounds like some awful orgy.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.670
And, um, and, uh, my wife

00:03:58.737 --> 00:04:01.808
was like at the time going,
this is -- this sucks.

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:04.243
You know, usually
the bells strike

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.745
and we all get to kiss
at 12:00, you know?

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.214
But we were all outside

00:04:08.281 --> 00:04:10.283
and we missed each other,
so all the women were together.

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:13.019
All the -- It was segregation,
basically segregation.

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:14.553
-I like that all the women
were telling her, like,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:15.888
I know this seems bad now,
but don't worry,

00:04:15.954 --> 00:04:18.224
James will be back soon
and he'll have coal.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:19.559
-He'll have coal.

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:21.260
-I don't know.
what's the name of --

00:04:21.327 --> 00:04:22.361
What's the name
of New Year's Eve in Scotland?

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:23.896
They just call it New Year's?

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.497
-No, we call it Hogmanay.
-Hogmanay.

00:04:25.564 --> 00:04:27.533
-It's spelt Hogmanay.

00:04:27.599 --> 00:04:31.837
So extend the "nay" as in,
like you're going "naaay", um.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.840
And -- And it's spelt "hog.'
But we say it "hug" -- Hogmanay.

00:04:34.907 --> 00:04:36.175
-Hogmanay.
-Hogmanay.

00:04:36.241 --> 00:04:38.210
-And, uh, and that's been around
for a long time.

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:39.512
I mean, it doesn't seem
like first footing

00:04:39.579 --> 00:04:41.114
is like a modern invention.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.449
-No, no, it's fairly old.
I don't know why we call it

00:04:43.515 --> 00:04:45.051
Hogmanay,
but, um, we call it Hogmanay.

00:04:45.117 --> 00:04:46.752
-I will say earlier today,
uh,

00:04:46.819 --> 00:04:49.054
you know, my segment producer
came in and said,

00:04:49.121 --> 00:04:51.190
"McAvoy is spinning this tale
